FILE regress/gdrom_decline_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: #
3: #  #490: the DECLINE detector for dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c's two de-fatalised sites.
4: #
5: #      Site A   case 0x30, an unimplemented data format      (GDROM_W_FMT)
6: #      Site B   default:, an unimplemented command byte      (GDROM_W_CMD)
7: #
8: #  Both were `fatal()` + `exit(1)` reached by an ordinary guest store, so a guest chose
9: #  whether the emulator kept running.  Both now decline, announced ONCE per device instance
10: #  behind their own latch bit, and `break` to the common tail.
11: #
12: #  *** WHY THIS IS A NEW PROBE RATHER THAN ROWS BOLTED ONTO gdrom_default_probe.py. ***
13: #  #489's precedent, and it is about coupling rather than tidiness: gdrom_default's
14: #  denominator is pinned as a literal in gate_sh_rounding.sh AND spelled out as a matching
15: #  `for v in ...` name list, so every added row edits two places that must agree.  A separate
16: #  probe with its own section and its own denominator has no such coupling.  Only the rows
17: #  that must observe the EXISTING stale session stayed there.
18: #
19: #  *** THIS PARAGRAPH SAID "the 58-row probe" AND "a 58-name list" TWICE.  gdrom_default is
20: #  now SIXTY -- this very round added `stale quiet` and `stale how` to it.  Corrected
21: #  2026-08-29; the count is deliberately no longer written here at all. ***  Naming a
22: #  sibling's row count in THIS file is the coupling the paragraph is arguing against, and it
23: #  went stale in the same round that wrote it.
24: #
25: #  AND THE COUNT HAS NO SOUND STATIC DERIVER, which is why it must not be transcribed:
26: #  `grep -c '^row('` on gdrom_default gives 52 and any-`row(` gives 55, because some rows are
27: #  emitted from loops.  **The MEASURED `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=60/60` is the only authority.**  This
28: #  probe is the easy case -- `^row(` = 20, agreeing with its measured 20/20 -- and that
29: #  difference between two sibling files is exactly why a count needs its deriver named.
30: #
31: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
32: #  *** THE CONTRACT: WHAT THIS DETECTOR IS DESIGNED TO CATCH, so a later escape is either
33: #  outside it or a breach of it rather than an argument about overclaiming. ***
34: #
35: #    1. reinstating either exit(1)                         -- `fmt surv` / `unk surv`
36: #    2. the decline not announcing at all                  -- `fmt says` / `unk says`
37: #    3. the announce losing its once-only latch            -- `fmt x2`  / `unk x2`
38: #    4. the two sites sharing ONE latch bit                -- `both sites`
39: #    5. either latch bit colliding WITH `GDROM_W_TOCLEN`    -- `crossbit`
40: #    6. `break` becoming `return` (skipping the tail)      -- `fmt cnt` / `unk cnt`,
41: #                                                             `fmt event` / `unk event`
42: #    7. the `break` slipping INSIDE the latch `if`         -- `latch cond`
43: #    8. the guard growing to swallow a legal format        -- `acceptA`
44: #    9. the upstream 0x24 arm being folded into a decline  -- `fmt 0x24`
45: #
46: #  *** CLAIM 5 READ "COLLIDING WITH AN EXISTING BIT" AND THAT OVERCLAIMED BY A FACTOR OF
47: #  SEVEN.  Narrowed 2026-08-29 after a pass-2 seat named the escape. ***  `crossbit` stages
48: #  only a TOC decline, so it can see a collision with `GDROM_W_TOCLEN` and NOTHING ELSE.
49: #  Aliasing either new bit onto any of the other six -- e.g.
50: #  `#define GDROM_W_FMT (1u << GDROM_W_OVERREAD_B)` -- is PREDICTED to score 20/20, because
51: #  no decline session performs an overread, a length mismatch or a DMA arm first, so the
52: #  aliased bit is clear in every session and every latch behaves normally.
53: #
54: #  DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED BY BUILDING TWELVE MORE ALIAS MUTANTS: that fails the addition
55: #  licence's economics, and the enum-by-construction shape bounds the realistic edit to a
56: #  hand-written raw mask.  `Ma_toclen`/`Mb_toclen` prove the most-benign-traffic instance IS
57: #  caught.  The contract now claims what was measured and discloses the rest, which is what
58: #  clause (e) is for.
59: #
60: #  *** THREE MEASURED 20/20 ESCAPES, BUILT BY THE PASS-2 ASK-AGAIN.  Disclosed 2026-08-29;
61: #  artifacts/gdromexitsix/p2askagain_20260829/, every log sentinel-verified, each mutant's
62: #  20-row block BYTE-IDENTICAL to base -- total escapes, not ties. ***
63: #
64: #    E1_aliasover  (1u << GDROM_W_FMT_B) -> (1u << GDROM_W_OVERREAD_B)          20/20
65: #    E2_assignA    `d->warned |= GDROM_W_FMT;` -> `d->warned = GDROM_W_FMT;`    20/20
66: #    E3_widenA     `& GDROM_W_FMT` -> `& (GDROM_W_FMT | GDROM_W_CMD)`           20/20
67: #
68: #  **E2 IS ONE CHARACTER AND IT IS THE WORST OF THE THREE.**  Site A still announces exactly
69: #  once, so every Site-A row stays green -- but the ASSIGNMENT clears the other eight latches.
70: #  A guest alternating a short TOC with a bad format then re-announces #489's diagnostic
71: #  WITHOUT BOUND, and `fatal()` cannot be silenced by `-q`.  **No row observes any latch
72: #  ACROSS a Site-A decline**, which is the structural gap: every latch row checks its OWN bit.
73: #
74: #  **E3 IS INVISIBLE ONLY BECAUSE OF ROW ORDER.**  Both two-site rows run Site A before
75: #  Site B, so widening Site A's test is unobservable; *the same edit at Site B WOULD be
76: #  caught* (`1,0`).  And pass 1's `S3 both` ran the OPPOSITE order -- the shipped rows
77: #  inverted it and nothing recorded that.
78: #
79: #  SEVERITY: **coverage debt, not a floor breach**, per #464's precedent (`wdcperopcode`,
80: #  `wdcsectorreset` -- the latter is E2's structural twin).  The floor's four clauses are all
81: #  measured clean.  These are hypothetical future edits in uncovered cells, which is exactly
82: #  what clause (e) exists to make FALSIFIABLE rather than argued.
83: #
84: #  A ONE-DECLARATION FIX FOR THE E1 CLASS EXISTS AND WAS CONTROLLED IN FIVE STATES -- a
85: #  compile-time disjointness assert in this file's own `gdrom_w_fits_its_carriers` idiom:
86: #  correct COMPILES; E1's alias REFUSED; `Ma_toclen`'s shape REFUSED; `M3shared`'s shape
87: #  REFUSED; and E1 WITHOUT the assert COMPILES (the negative control).  **The addition-licence
88: #  objection that declined the twelve alias mutants does NOT apply to a compile-time bound**,
89: #  which costs no rows and fails CLOSED when a tenth bit arrives.  Filed for the owner.
90: #
91: #  NOT claimed:
92: #    * collision with the other six latch bits -- see above, and now MEASURED as E1;
93: #    * any latch's state ACROSS a decline at the other site (E2);
94: #    * a latch test widened to cover a sibling bit at Site A (E3, order-dependent);
95: #    * the tail's THIRD channel.  The added comments say the tail "zeroes d->cnt, clears
96: #      COND_DATA_AVAIL and fires the completion event".  `cnt` has `fmt cnt`/`unk cnt` and
97: #      the event has `fmt event`/`unk event`; **the COND_DATA_AVAIL clear has NO ROW.**  It
98: #      is guest-observable through the `cond` read arm, and a row is buildable (stage
99: #      DATA_AVAIL with a good TOC, decline, read cond).  It is NOT owed under the row-demand
100: #      bound -- both `M_ret*` mutants are already killed twice over -- so this sentence is
101: #      the explicit no-row discharge the CLAIM->ROW check requires, not an omission;
102: #    * anything about the three exit(1) sites still live in dev_dreamcast_gdrom_access(),
103: #      which are a different round; and nothing about exploitability of the heap overflow
104: #      #489's guard prevents -- see artifacts/gdromexitsix/asan_m0_20260828/.
105: #
106: #  *** A ROW THAT FAILS **OPEN**, recorded because it is invisible in a green table. ***
107: #  `latch cond` wants 0 and reads `REJ`, the unlatched `rejecting sector_count` diagnostic --
108: #  which is OUTSIDE this diff, guest-drivable, and a natural future target of this very
109: #  de-fatalisation campaign.  Reword that text and the regex matches nothing, the row goes
110: #  permanently and silently GREEN, and no other row reads `REJ` with a non-zero want.  `HUH`
315:                                                    A.take(1)) + ["print r%d" % dest]
316: 
317: 
318: def packet(words, A):
319:     """Arm the device and push a 12-byte command packet, low halfword first."""
320:     out = store16(GDROM_COND, 0xa0, A)
321:     for w in words:
322:         out += store16(GDROM_DATA, w, A)
323:     return out
324: 
325: 
326: #  A packet is SIX halfwords and dispatch fires on the sixth.  cmd[0] is the LOW byte of
327: #  word 0, so 0x2030 means cmd[0]=0x30, cmd[1]=0x20 -- a pass-1 seat read this backwards and
328: #  its conclusion happened to survive, which is why the byte order is stated here.
329: def cmd(c0, c1=0x00, c10=0x0000):
330:     return [c1 * 256 + c0, 0, 0, 0, 0, c10]
331: 
332: 
333: GOOD_FMT = cmd(0x30, 0x20)       # the legal read-sectors packet
334: BAD_FMT = cmd(0x30, 0x21)        # Site A: an unimplemented data format
335: FMT_24 = cmd(0x30, 0x24)         # the upstream warn-and-continue arm -- NOT a decline
336: BAD_CMD = cmd(0x31)              # Site B: an unimplemented command byte
337: MOUNT = cmd(0x70)                # implemented, allocates nothing
338: TOC_SHORT = [0x0014, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]   # a TOC whose cnt will be wrong -> #489's decline
339: 
340: 
341: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity controls
342: #  A detector that cannot fail on a null medium is vacuous.  These three fail on a stale
343: #  copy, a comment-only file, or a binary that never reached the device.
344: 
345: A = Addr(CODE)
346: t, alive, how = session(["r7=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r8=0x%08x" % LIVE]
347:                         + run([MOVL_ST(7, 8)], A.take(1))
348:                         + ["r1=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r2=0"]
349:                         + run([0x6002 | (1 << 4) | (2 << 8)], A.take(1)) + ["print r2"])
350: row("live", printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died", "0x%x" % LIVE,
351:     "LIVENESS: a guest store then a guest load, through RAM")
352: 
353: #  *** A 32-BIT LOAD, MASKED -- NOT A 16-BIT ONE.  MEASURED: the 16-bit form reads 0x0
354: #  and this control FAILED on the probe's first run. ***  SH4_IPRD does not serve a
355: #  halfword access here, so `mov.w` returns nothing and the row cannot tell a live
356: #  device from a dead one -- a CONTROL that fails open is worse than no control.
357: #  Copied from gdrom_toclen_probe.py, which had already solved it.
358: #
359: #  *** THIS IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS FILE I INVENTED A VARIANT OF A SOLVED PROBLEM
360: #  RATHER THAN COPYING THE WORKING ONE *** -- after a bogus write-1-to-clear pre-clear
361: #  on the event rows, and a summed pair on `fmt 0x24`.  The sibling probe is the
362: #  reference implementation; deviate from it only with a reason worth writing down.
363: A = Addr(CODE)
364: t, alive, how = session(load32(0xffd00010, A))
365: g = printed(t, "r2")
366: row("devsig sh4", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xffff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
367:     "0x%x" % IPRD_RESET, "DEVSIG: the SH4 IPRD reset value; RAM would read 0")
368: 
369: A = Addr(CODE)
370: t, alive, how = session(load16(GDROM_BUSY, A))
371: g = printed(t, "r2")
372: row("gdrom map", ("mapped" if g.startswith("0x") else g) if alive else "died", "mapped",
373:     "the GDROM window is served, not a paddr fault")
374: 
375: #  *** CONTROL IS EMITTED HERE, OVER THE THREE IDENTITY ROWS ONLY -- NOT AT THE END OVER
376: #  EVERYTHING.  MEASURED WHY ON THIS PROBE'S FIRST RUN: it printed CONTROL=DEAD because ONE
377: #  PRODUCT ROW FAILED, which is exactly the conflation the gate section exists to prevent. ***
378: #  The gate reads this to say "a run whose controls died is an APPARATUS failure and must not
379: #  be read as product failures".  A CONTROL line that also goes DEAD on a product failure
380: #  makes that sentence false and sends a reader hunting a broken rig for a real defect.
381: #  `rows and` is load-bearing too: all([]) is True, so an empty list would certify a run that
382: #  measured nothing.
383: print("GDROM_DECL_CONTROL=%s"
384:       % ("OK" if rows and all(ok for _, ok in rows) else "DEAD"))
385: 
386: 
387: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- SITE A: the format decline
388: #  kills: reinstating exit(1) at Site A          via: how
389: #  kills: the arm not declining at all           via: the anchored decline text
390: A = Addr(CODE)
391: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A))
392: row("fmt surv", how, "exit0", "a bad data format must not end the host")
393: row("fmt says", "%d" % len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
394:     "and it announces itself, exactly once")
395: 
396: #  kills: M_braceA -- `break` moved INSIDE the latch `if`, so the SECOND identical bad
397: #         format falls through into sector_nr.  MEASURED escaping a 13-row plan, because
398: #         "announces once" reads 1 either way.
399: #  via:   a DECLINE channel read after the SECOND command, not the announce count
400: A = Addr(CODE)
401: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A) + packet(BAD_FMT, A))
402: row("fmt x2", "%d" % len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
403:     "two bad formats, ONE line -- the latch holds")
404: row("latch cond", "%d" % len(REJ.findall(t)), "0",
405:     "and the SECOND one still declines -- no fallthrough to sector_nr")
406: 
407: #  kills: M_retA -- `break` -> `return`, which skips the common tail.
408: #  via:   d->cnt, which the tail zeroes.  *** STAGED WITH NO PRIOR DISPATCH: the CNT channel
409: #         discriminates ONLY when d->cnt is non-zero on entry, and M_retA measured CNT=0x0
410: #         (escaping) in a session where an earlier command had already zeroed it. ***
411: #         pending_irq is STICKY, so the same single-command staging is what `fmt event`
412: #         needs -- a staging TOC would pre-set the bit and make that row vacuous.
413: A = Addr(CODE)
414: #  *** `fmt cnt` WANTS 0x0 -- AND A FAILED READ ALSO GIVES 0x0.  So the staging is read
415: #  BACK IN THE SAME SESSION FIRST: without `fmt stage` proving 0x98 actually landed,
416: #  `fmt cnt` is green whether the tail zeroed the count or the read simply did not
417: #  work.  The pair is the oracle; neither half is one alone. ***
418: t, alive, how = session(store16(GDROM_CNTLO, 0x98, A) + store16(GDROM_CNTHI, 0x01, A)
419:                         + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A, dest=3)
420:                         + packet(BAD_FMT, A) + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A))
421: row("fmt stage", printed(t, "r3"), "0x98",
422:     "the staged count is really there BEFORE the command")
423: row("fmt cnt", printed(t, "r2"), "0x0",
424:     "and the tail zeroed it -- a return would have left 0x98")
425: 
426: A = Addr(CODE)
427: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A) + load32(SYSASIC_PEND1, A))
428: g = printed(t, "r2")
429: row("fmt event", ("set" if (int(g, 16) & SYSASIC_GDROM_BIT) else "clear")
430:     if g.startswith("0x") else g, "set",
431:     "a declined command still reports completion -- a return would hang the guest")
432: 
433: #  *** NO PRE-CLEAR OF THE PENDING REGISTER, AND THE OBVIOUS 'CAREFUL' FORM IS WRONG. ***
434: #  An earlier draft of these two rows wrote 0 to SYSASIC_PEND1 first, to clear the bit
435: #  before the command set it.  `dev_dreamcast_asic.c`'s write arm is
436: #  `d->pending_irq[r] &= ~idata` -- **WRITE-1-TO-CLEAR** -- so writing 0 clears NOTHING.
437: #  The bit would never have been cleared, and the row would pass whether or not the
438: #  decline fired the event: VACUOUS, and vacuous in the direction that looks diligent.
439: #  It was also a 16-bit store to a 32-bit register.  The correct construction needs no
440: #  clear at all: each session is a FRESH process, so the bit starts clear and only the
441: #  dispatched command can set it.  Copied from gdrom_toclen_probe.py's `bad event`,
442: #  which had already solved this.  Found by desk-check against the device source
443: #  before this file was ever run.
444: #  kills: an accept-set growth that folds the upstream 0x24 arm into the decline.
445: #  via:   the 0x24 line is byte-identical upstream text and must match NEITHER new regex.
446: A = Addr(CODE)
447: t, alive, how = session(packet(FMT_24, A))
448: #  *** ASSERT THE PAIR, NOT THE SUM.  An earlier draft summed the two counts and wanted
449: #  1 -- which is BLIND TO EXACTLY THE MUTANT IT NAMES: folding 0x24 into the decline
450: #  converts one match into the other and the SUM STAYS 1.  A sum hides a swap. ***
451: row("fmt 0x24", "%d,%d" % (len(FMT_0X24.findall(t)), len(FMT_DECL.findall(t))), "1,0",
452:     "0x24 keeps its own unlatched line and is NOT this site's decline")
453: 
454: 
455: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- SITE B: the command decline
456: A = Addr(CODE)
457: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_CMD, A))
458: row("unk surv", how, "exit0", "an unknown command must not end the host")
459: row("unk says", "%d" % len(CMD_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
460:     "and it announces itself, exactly once")
461: 
462: #  kills: M2nolatch -- the bit tested but never set, so every occurrence announces.
463: A = Addr(CODE)
464: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_CMD, A) + packet(BAD_CMD, A))
465: row("unk x2", "%d" % len(CMD_DECL.findall(t)), "1",
466:     "two unknown commands, ONE line -- the latch holds")
467: 
468: #  kills: M_retA at Site B.  Same staging rule as `fmt cnt`.
469: A = Addr(CODE)
470: t, alive, how = session(store16(GDROM_CNTLO, 0x98, A) + store16(GDROM_CNTHI, 0x01, A)
471:                         + packet(BAD_CMD, A) + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A))
472: row("unk cnt", printed(t, "r2"), "0x0", "the tail zeroed the count")
473: 
474: A = Addr(CODE)
475: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_CMD, A) + load32(SYSASIC_PEND1, A))
476: g = printed(t, "r2")
477: row("unk event", ("set" if (int(g, 16) & SYSASIC_GDROM_BIT) else "clear")
478:     if g.startswith("0x") else g, "set", "the declined command reports completion")
479: 
480: 
481: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the two-site rows
482: #  kills: M3shared -- both sites folded onto ONE latch bit.  MEASURED escaping: the second
483: #         site falls silent because the first already latched.  ORTHOGONAL to `unk x2` and
484: #         `fmt x2` -- neither of those sees it, and it does not see them.
485: A = Addr(CODE)
486: t, alive, how = session(packet(BAD_FMT, A) + packet(BAD_CMD, A))
487: row("both sites", "%d,%d" % (len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), len(CMD_DECL.findall(t))), "1,1",
488:     "each site speaks for itself -- separate latch bits")
489: 
490: #  kills: Ma_toclen / Mb_toclen -- either new bit COLLIDING with an existing one.
491: #  *** THE WORST OF THE MEASURED ESCAPES.  d->warned has NINE writers sharing one field and
492: #  SIX of the pre-existing ones are set from inside DEVICE_ACCESS by ordinary guest register
493: #  reads, so a collision is silenced by traffic that looks entirely normal. ***  A short TOC
494: #  sets GDROM_W_TOCLEN first; if either new bit aliases it, that site prints NOTHING.
495: A = Addr(CODE)
496: t, alive, how = session(packet(TOC_SHORT, A) + packet(BAD_FMT, A) + packet(BAD_CMD, A))
497: row("crossbit", "%d,%d" % (len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), len(CMD_DECL.findall(t))), "1,1",
498:     "a prior TOC decline must not silence either new site")
499: 
500: 
501: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the accept side
502: #  The de-escalation clause: a fatal->survive fix DELETES the accidental tripwire that made
503: #  guard growth self-announcing.  Before this round, widening `!= 0x20` meant exit(1) on the
504: #  first legal write -- unmissable.  After it, the same edit means a silently refused write.
505: #  kills: M_growA (`!= 0x20` -> `!= 0x21`), MEASURED giving huh 1->0.
506: A = Addr(CODE)
507: t, alive, how = session(store16(GDROM_CNTLO, 0x98, A) + store16(GDROM_CNTHI, 0x01, A)
508:                         + packet(cmd(0x30, 0x20, 0x0001), A))
509: row("acceptA", "%d" % len(HUH.findall(t)), "1",
510:     "the LEGAL format still enters the arm -- growth would silence this")
511: row("accept sil", "%d" % len(FMT_DECL.findall(t)), "0",
512:     "and it is not declined")
513: 
514: 
515: n = sum(1 for _, ok in rows if ok)
516: print("GDROM_DECL_RESULT=%d/%d" % (n, len(rows)))
517: print("GDROM_DECL_VECTOR=" + ",".join(("1" if ok else "0") for _, ok in rows))
518: sys.exit(0 if n == len(rows) else 1)

FILE regress/pcc2_width_witness.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """pcc2widthkills -- RUNG-3 WITNESS for the access-width host kills in dev_pcc2.c.
3: 
4: DEFECT.  DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2) has TEN register arms of the shape
5: 
6:     case PCCTWO_GENCTL:
7:             if (len != 1) {
8:                     fatal("TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_GENCTL\\n");
9:                     exit(1);
10:             }
11: 
12: A guest choosing an access WIDTH ends the host process.  The killing widths are 2
13: and 4 -- ordinary, naturally alignable -- so a plain aligned halfword load of a
14: documented register is enough.  Same class as #337 (dev_wdc.c) and #497 (dev_sgi_re.c).
15: 
16: *** THE COUNT IS TEN, NOT TWELVE. ***  OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md's row says "twelve ... lines
17: 359, 371, 394, 407, 429, 467, 477, 492, 507, 528, 543, 563".  Lines 543 and 563 are the
18: PCCTWO_IPL and PCCTWO_MASK arms, and both are ALREADY #119 warn-once latches, not kills.
19: The adjudication's "5 of the 10" is the right denominator.  Records defect, noted here so
20: the round's own record does not copy the twelve forward.
21: 
22: REACHABILITY, from OB:widthkillclass (2026-08-30), verified by reading
23: cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c: every NON-byte m88k access checks alignment BEFORE
24: memory_rw and raises MISALIGNED_ACCESS, and `relative_addr %= PCC2_SIZE' (0x40) keeps an
25: odd offset odd in all 64 aliases.  So only the EVEN-offset arms are guest-reachable:
26: 
27:     REACHABLE (5)     GENCTL 0x02   T2CTL 0x16   T2ICR 0x1a   SCCERR 0x1c   SCCTX 0x1e
28:     UNREACHABLE (7)   VECBASE 0x03  PSCALEADJ 0x15  T1CTL 0x17  T1ICR 0x1b  SCCICR 0x1d
29:                       SCCRX 0x1f  SCSIICR 0x2f    (odd -- a GUEST non-byte access cannot
30:                                                  land.  The DEBUGGER's `put h' warns and
31:                                                  stores anyway -- MEASURED, it killed --
32:                                                  so they are OPERATOR-reachable and the
33:                                                  detector reaches them that way.)
34: 
35:     and of the five, only SCCERR 0x1c is 4-aligned, so a WORD access reaches one arm
36:     and a HALFWORD access reaches all five.
37: 
38: RUNG 3: real MC88100 instructions (ld.b / ld.h / ld / st.h / st.b) executed by the CPU
39: through real address decode and real memory_rw, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree
40: `-E mvme88k -e mvme187' (the only machine that instantiates pcc2, at PCC2_BASE 0xfff42000).
41: *** THE FIRST RUN PASSED `-e 187' AND EVERY ROW WENT RED, INCLUDING THE PURE-RAM CONTROL --
42: which is the control doing its job: "Unknown subtype '187'", exit 1, no session ever
43: started.  0/10 was the witness failing for the WRONG reason, and mechanical rule 4 says
44: do not claim it.  The subtype alias is `mvme187'. ***
45: No mvme88k rig exists here, so this is the strongest witness available; boot is not.
46: 
47: *** FIRST CORRECTED RUN: 3/10 -- controls green, three kills red FOR THE REASON UNDER
48: TEST, and one row red for MY reason: the liveness loads read a scratch word that no
49: instruction in THAT session had stored (each session is a fresh emulator), so they read
50: 0 instead of 0x5a5a5a5a.  r4 was preset to a sentinel, so 0 vs sentinel vs absent were
51: still three distinguishable states -- but the row's note said "runs on to the RAM load"
52: and its want assumed a store the program did not contain.  Every kill session now
53: STORES the scratch word before the killing access. ***
54: 
55: TEST-FIRST SHAPE: every `want' below is the FIXED behaviour.  On the committed pre-fix
56: build the kill rows red (how == exit1); after the fix the same script goes green.
57: 
58: CONTROLS, clause (ii) of the witness ladder:
59:     live     a RAM word round-trips through the same decode
60:     devsig   a byte WRITE to CHIPID makes the DEVICE speak ("IGNORING write to read-only
61:              PCCTWO_CHIPID/CHIPREV") -- RAM emits no text, ever.  Independent of the
62:              width path, so it controls for "a device is there" without borrowing the
63:              channel under test.
64:     byte rd  the LEGAL width (ld.b) at the same register the kill row uses -- must be
65:              serviced on BOTH builds, so a fix that declines too much reds it.
66: 
67: ENCODING (immediate form): op26<<26 | rD<<21 | rS1<<16 | imm16.  Derived from the tree's
68: own constants -- m88k_idle_probe.py's `ld r13,r2,0' is 0x15a20000, i.e. op26 0x05, and
69: cpu_m88k_instr.c's dispatch gives ld.h 0x06, ld.b 0x07, st 0x09, st.h 0x0a, st.b 0x0b.
70: 
71: Sentinel: PCC2_WIDTH_WITNESS_DONE
72: """
73: 
74: import os, pty, re, select, sys, time
75: 
76: if len(sys.argv) < 2:
77:     sys.stderr.write("usage: pcc2_width_witness.py <gxemul> [label]\n")
78:     sys.exit(2)
79: 
80: BIN = sys.argv[1]
81: LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "local"
82: 
83: DUMMY = "/tmp/pcc2_dummy.bin"
84: open(DUMMY, "wb").write(b"\x00\x00\x00\x00")
85: 
86: CODE = 0x00010000            # guest code page
87: SCRATCH = 0x00020000         # RAM scratch, a DIFFERENT page from the code
88: PCC2 = 0xfff42000            # PCC2_BASE, mvme_pcctworeg.h
89: GENCTL, T2CTL, SCCERR, CHIPID = 0x02, 0x16, 0x1c, 0x00
90: 
91: #  r2 = PCC2 base, r3 = scratch, r4 = load target, r5 = store source.
92: def enc(op26, rd, rs1, imm):
93:     return (op26 << 26) | (rd << 21) | (rs1 << 16) | (imm & 0xffff)
94: 
95: I_ST_W_R5_R3   = enc(0x09, 5, 3, 0)          # st   r5,r3,0      RAM
96: I_LD_W_R4_R3   = enc(0x05, 4, 3, 0)          # ld   r4,r3,0      RAM
97: I_ST_B_R5_R2_0 = enc(0x0b, 5, 2, CHIPID)     # st.b r5,r2,0x00   devsig (write-ignored msg)
98: I_LD_B_R4_R2_2 = enc(0x07, 4, 2, GENCTL)     # ld.b r4,r2,0x02   legal width, control
99: I_LD_H_R4_R2_2 = enc(0x06, 4, 2, GENCTL)     # ld.h r4,r2,0x02   *** KILL: halfword read
100: I_LD_W_R4_R2_1C = enc(0x05, 4, 2, SCCERR)    # ld   r4,r2,0x1c   *** KILL: word read
158:     try:
159:         os.close(fd)
160:     except OSError:
161:         pass
162:     how = "nostatus"
163:     try:
164:         _, stat = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
165:         if os.WIFSIGNALED(stat):
166:             how = "sig%d" % os.WTERMSIG(stat)
167:         elif os.WIFEXITED(stat):
168:             how = "exit%d" % os.WEXITSTATUS(stat)
169:     except OSError:
170:         pass
171:     return buf.replace("\r", ""), alive, how
172: 
173: 
174: def printed(t, reg):
175:     m = re.search(r"print\s+%s\s*\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
176:     return "0x%x" % int(m.group(1), 16) if m else "absent"
177: 
178: 
179: def program(insns, at=CODE):
180:     """put the instruction words + a self-loop, point pc at them, step past them."""
181:     words = list(insns) + [I_BR_SELF, 0]
182:     cmds = ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * i, w) for i, w in enumerate(words)]
183:     return cmds + ["pc=0x%x" % at, "step %d" % len(insns)]
184: 
185: 
186: SETUP = ["r2=0x%x" % PCC2, "r3=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r5=0x5a5a5a5a", "r4=0x%x" % SENTINEL]
187: 
188: print("pcc2widthkills RUNG-3 WITNESS -- binary=%s label=%s" % (BIN, LABEL))
189: print("%-16s %-12s %-12s %-46s %s" % ("row", "got", "want", "note", ""))
190: 
191: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- controls
192: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_W_R5_R3, I_LD_W_R4_R3]) + ["print r4"])
193: row("live", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
194:     "RAM word round-trips through the same decode")
195: 
196: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_B_R5_R2_0]))
197: row("devsig", t.count(DEVSIG), 1,
198:     "a byte write to CHIPID makes the DEVICE speak -- RAM emits no text")
199: 
200: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_W_R5_R3, I_LD_B_R4_R2_2, I_LD_W_R4_R3]) + ["print r4"])
201: row("byte rd", how, "exit0",
202:     "ld.b of GENCTL is the LEGAL width -- serviced on both builds")
203: row("byte rd liv", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
204:     "...and the guest runs on to the RAM load")
205: 
206: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the kills
207: #  Each kill row is PAIRED with a positive post-access observable, because exit
208: #  status alone cannot tell "declined and quit" from "died with status 0".
209: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_W_R5_R3, I_LD_H_R4_R2_2, I_LD_W_R4_R3]) + ["print r4"])
210: row("half rd", how, "exit0",
211:     "ld.h of GENCTL (aligned halfword READ) must not end the host")
212: row("half rd liv", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
213:     "...and the guest runs on -- kills a death however it is spelled")
214: 
215: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_W_R5_R3, I_LD_W_R4_R2_1C, I_LD_W_R4_R3]) + ["print r4"])
216: row("word rd", how, "exit0",
217:     "ld of SCCERR (the one 4-aligned arm) must not end the host")
218: row("word rd liv", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
219:     "...and the guest runs on")
220: 
221: t, alive, how = session(SETUP + program([I_ST_W_R5_R3, I_ST_H_R5_R2_16, I_LD_W_R4_R3]) + ["print r4"])
222: row("half wr", how, "exit0",
223:     "st.h to T2CTL (aligned halfword WRITE) must not end the host")
224: row("half wr liv", printed(t, "r4") if alive else "died", "0x5a5a5a5a",
225:     "...and the guest runs on")
226: 
227: bad = [n for n, ok in rows if not ok]
228: print("PCC2W_RESULT=%d/%d" % (len(rows) - len(bad), len(rows)))
229: print("PCC2W_LABEL=%s" % LABEL)
230: if bad:
231:     print("PCC2W_RED=%s" % ",".join(bad))
232: print("PCC2_WIDTH_WITNESS_DONE")

FILE regress/gdrom_toclen_probe.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """DETECTOR for `gdromexitsix` Round 1 -- the `case 0x14` TOC length guard in
3: dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c (`grep -n 'Read TOC with' <file>`).
4: 
5: The WITNESS is a separate, ungated file: artifacts/gdromexitsix/gdrom_toclen_witness.py,
6: whose badlen/zerolen rows pinned `exit1` and were MEASURED RED on the committed build at
7: 84f6682 (repro_20260828/PREFIX_committed_84f6682.out).  This file pins the REPAIRED
8: behaviour.  The two exist separately, rather than one file being re-pinned, because a
9: witness goes red the day its fix lands and gating one manufactures a regression.
10: 
11: DETECTOR CONTRACT (vacuity floor clause (e)) -- what these rows are designed to catch,
12: stated so a later escape is either "outside the contract" or "a breach of it" rather than
13: an argument about overclaiming:
14: 
15:   * REINSTATEMENT of the host kill at the TOC length guard, in any form that ends the
16:     process -- the `bad surv` and `zero surv` rows;
17:   * a guard whose REPAIR IS NULLED so a short cnt reaches alloc_data() again -- the
18:     `bad cond` row, which is the clause-(d) carrier and the reason this probe reads
19:     GDROM_COND at all;
20:   * loss of the ACCEPT side, i.e. a guard that grows and starts refusing cnt == 408 --
21:     the `ok data0`/`ok data1`/`ok cond` rows;
22:   * loss of the once-latch on the decline diagnostic -- the `latch once` row;
23:   * a decline that RETURNS instead of BREAKING, skipping the common tail -- the
24:     `bad cnt` and `bad event` rows, added after that edit was MEASURED to escape
25:     an earlier 13-row version of this file 13/13;
26:   * a guard that EXEMPTS one end of the refused range -- `zero cond` (the
27:     exempt-zero form, which re-opens the heap overflow at cnt=0) and `over cond`
28:     (the `< 408` form, which admits over-length requests).  Both were predicted
29:     to sweep the 15-row version CLEAN, by three seats on pass 2.
30: 
31:   *** THE SHAPE THAT KEEPS RECURRING HERE, STATED ONCE: A SURVIVE-ONLY ROW IS
32:   VACUOUS AT THIS SITE. ***  Three separate escapes -- `> 408`, exempt-zero, and
33:   `< 408` -- all leave the host alive while the guard is wrong, because the
34:   damage is either a non-faulting overwrite or no damage at all.  Every value the
35:   guard refuses therefore needs a DECLINE-CHANNEL row (COND stays 0xa0), not just
36:   a survival row.  `bad surv`, `zero surv` and `over surv` are kept as companions,
37:   but none of them is the row that kills anything.
38: 
39:   NOT claimed: that a one-BYTE overflow is detected by crashing.  It is not; the
40:   `> 408` mutant is caught by the COND channel, not by a fault.  See the note on
41:   `bad cond` below -- that is the whole reason a survive-only row set was refused.
42: 
43:   NOT claimed: coverage of edits to the TOC WRITER itself (the hardcoded indices),
44:   or of any arm other than `case 0x14`.  The other `exit(1)` sites in this file
45:   are gdromexitsix Round 2 and the open `gdromcondlies` row.
46: 
47: WHY A SURVIVE-ONLY ROW SET WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, measured rather than assumed.
48: The obvious clause-(d) mutant weakens `!= 408` to `> 408`.  Then cnt=407 falls THROUGH to
49: alloc_data(407), and the writer's hardcoded indices run to byte 407 -- a one-byte heap
50: overflow that a normal -O3 build does not necessarily fault on.  A row that only asks
51: "did the host survive?" scores that mutant GREEN while the defect the round closed is
52: back.  The discriminator is a SECOND OBSERVABLE CHANNEL:
53: 
54:     cnt == 408 accepted  ->  d->data != NULL  ->  tail sets COND_DATA_AVAIL   -> 0xa8
55:     cnt != 408 declined  ->  d->data == NULL  ->  tail CLEARS it, cnt = 0     -> 0xa0
56: 
57: Both values are DERIVED, not fitted: the guest writes 0xa0 to GDROM_COND to start the
58: command, and the common tail either ORs in COND_DATA_AVAIL (0x08) or masks it out.  The
59: `case GDROM_COND:` read arm returns `d->cond` unmodified.
60: 
61: RUNG 3: every access is a real guest instruction through real address decode and real
62: memory_rw, on the UNMODIFIED committed `dreamcast` machine description.  Nothing is
63: device_add'ed and no machine is introduced to reach the site.
64: 
65: DREAMCAST NEEDS -x (two console inputs) or the session dies for a CONFIGURATION reason and
66: reports the same "died" a defect would.
67: 
68: USAGE:  gdrom_toclen_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <kernel-image> [extra args...]
69: """
70: import os
71: import pty
72: import re
73: import select
74: import sys
75: import time
76: 
77: BIN = sys.argv[1]
78: KERNEL = sys.argv[2]
79: EXTRA = sys.argv[3:]
80: 
81: CODE = 0x8c001000
82: SCRATCH = 0x8c001800
83: 
84: GDROM_DATA = 0xa05f7080
85: GDROM_BUSY = 0xa05f7018
86: GDROM_CNTLO = 0xa05f7090
87: GDROM_CNTHI = 0xa05f7094
88: GDROM_COND = 0xa05f709c
89: #  SYSASIC_EVENT_GDROM is 32 (thirdparty/dreamcast_sysasicvar.h), and the macro
90: #  addresses SYSASIC_BASE + 4*(e>>5) -- so event 32 lands in pending_irq[1], bit
232: def stage_toc(A, cnt):
233:     """CNTLO/CNTHI = cnt, COND = 0xa0, then the Read-TOC packet.  9 accesses."""
234:     return (store16(GDROM_CNTLO, cnt & 0xff, A) +
235:             store16(GDROM_CNTHI, (cnt >> 8) & 0xff, A) +
236:             store16(GDROM_COND, 0xa0, A) +
237:             packet(CMD_TOC, A))
238: 
239: 
240: # ------------------------------------------------------------------- controls
241: A = Addr(CODE)
242: t, alive, how = session(["r7=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r8=0x%08x" % LIVE,
243:                          "r1=0x%x" % SCRATCH, "r2=0"] +
244:                         run([MOVL_ST(7, 8), MOVL_LD(2, 1)], A.take()) +
245:                         ["print r2"])
246: row("live", printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died", "0x%x" % LIVE,
247:     "LIVENESS: guest store then guest load, through RAM")
248: 
249: A = Addr(CODE)
250: t, alive, how = session(["r1=0x%x" % SH4_IPRD, "r2=0"] +
251:                         run([MOVL_LD(2, 1)], A.take()) + ["print r2"])
252: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
253: row("devsig sh4", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xffff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
254:     "0x%x" % IPRD_RESET, "DEVSIG: SH4 IPRD reset value, RAM reads 0")
255: 
256: A = Addr(CODE)
257: t, alive, how = session(["r1=0x%x" % GDROM_BUSY, "r2=0"] +
258:                         run([MOVL_LD(2, 1)], A.take()) + ["print r2"])
259: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
260: row("gdrom map", ("mapped" if g.startswith("0x") else g) if alive else "died",
261:     "mapped", "GDROM_BUSY read is served, not a paddr fault")
262: print("TOCLEN_CONTROL=%s" % ("OK" if all(ok for _, ok in rows) else "DEAD"))
263: 
264: # ------------------------------------------------------------------- accept side
265: #
266: #  THE DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.  This round removed a host-fatal guard, which
267: #  deleted the accidental tripwire that made guard growth self-announcing: before
268: #  the fix, narrowing the accept set meant exit(1) on the first legal write.  These
269: #  rows pin the accept side so a grown guard reddens instead of going quiet.
270: A = Addr(CODE)
271: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 408) +
272:                         ["r1=0x%x" % GDROM_DATA, "r2=0", "r3=0"] +
273:                         run([MOVW_LD(2, 1), MOVW_LD(3, 1)], A.take()) +
274:                         ["print r2", "print r3"])
275: row("ok data0", printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died", "0x10",
276:     "ACCEPT: synthesized TOC halfword 0, device-only value")
277: row("ok data1", printed(t, "r3") if alive else "died", "0xffff9600",
278:     "ACCEPT: TOC halfword 1, sign-extended by mov.w")
279: row("ok surv", how, "exit0", "ACCEPT: the legal length must not kill the host")
280: 
281: A = Addr(CODE)
282: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 408) + load16(GDROM_COND, A))
283: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
284: row("ok cond", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
285:     "0xa8", "ACCEPT: 0xa0 written, COND_DATA_AVAIL(0x08) SET by the tail")
286: 
287: # ------------------------------------------------------------------- the decline
288: #
289: #  `bad surv`/`zero surv` are the reinstatement carriers: they pinned exit1 on the
290: #  committed build and pin exit0 now.
291: A = Addr(CODE)
292: t1, alive1, how1 = session(stage_toc(A, 407))
293: row("bad surv", how1, "exit0",
294:     "cnt=407 declines instead of ending the host")
295: row("bad says", "yes" if len(DECLINE.findall(t1)) >= 1 else "no", "yes",
296:     "the decline ANNOUNCES itself -- not a silent refusal")
297: 
298: A = Addr(CODE)
299: t2, alive2, how2 = session(stage_toc(A, 0))
300: row("zero surv", how2, "exit0",
301:     "cnt=0 declines too -- the malloc(0) end of the same guard")
302: 
303: #  *** THE CLAUSE-(d) CARRIER, and the reason this probe reads COND at all. ***
304: #  A guard weakened from `!= 408` to `> 408` lets cnt=407 reach alloc_data(407)
305: #  while the writer indexes byte 407 -- a one-byte heap overflow that need not
306: #  fault, so `bad surv` alone would score it GREEN.  Under that mutant d->data is
307: #  non-NULL, so the tail SETS COND_DATA_AVAIL and this row reads 0xa8, not 0xa0.
308: A = Addr(CODE)
309: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 407) + load16(GDROM_COND, A))
310: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
311: row("bad cond", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
312:     "0xa0", "DECLINE: COND_DATA_AVAIL stays CLEAR -- kills the `> 408` mutant")
313: 
314: #  *** THE break-vs-return ROWS.  ADDED AFTER A MEASURED L1: this detector scored
315: #  13/13 against `break;` -> `return;`, and the product comment beside the fix
316: #  CLAIMS that edit "would skip that event and leave the guest waiting forever"
317: #  while shipping no row for it.  That is the CLAIM -> ROW check, unrun, on the
318: #  round's own diff.  Found by the opus seat on Round 2's design panel. ***
319: #
320: #  *** AND `bad cond` STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE IT, which is worth stating because
321: #  it is the clause-(d) carrier and looks like it should. ***  The guest writes
322: #  0xa0 to start the command, and BIT 3 IS ALREADY CLEAR in 0xa0.  So the tail's
323: #  `d->cond &= ~COND_DATA_AVAIL` is a NO-OP on this path: `break` (tail runs,
324: #  clears an already-clear bit) and `return` (tail never runs) both leave 0xa0.
325: #  A row can only separate them by observing something the TAIL WRITES.
326: #
327: #  On the decline path the tail writes exactly two things -- `d->cnt = 0` and the
328: #  SYSASIC event -- so there is one row each, and between them they pin both
329: #  halves of what the comment claims.
330: A = Addr(CODE)
331: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 407) + load16(GDROM_CNTLO, A))
332: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
333: row("bad cnt", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
334:     "0x0", "DECLINE: the tail zeroed d->cnt -- `return` leaves 0x97")
335: 
336: #  The event itself, pinned directly rather than inferred.  This is the stronger
337: #  of the two: `bad cnt` shows the tail RAN, this shows the guest was actually
338: #  TOLD the command completed -- which is the property that stops a hang.
339: A = Addr(CODE)
340: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 407) +
341:                         ["r1=0x%x" % SYSASIC_PEND1, "r2=0"] +
342:                         run([MOVL_LD(2, 1)], A.take()) + ["print r2"])
343: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
344: row("bad event",
345:     ("set" if (int(g, 16) & SYSASIC_GDROM_BIT) else "clear")
346:     if g.startswith("0x") else g,
347:     "set", "DECLINE: SYSASIC_EVENT_GDROM fired -- `return` would skip it")
348: 
349: #  *** THE ZERO AND OVER-LENGTH COND ROWS.  ADDED AFTER PASS 2 MEASURED TWO MORE
350: #  ESCAPES, ONE OF WHICH RE-OPENS THE HEAP OVERFLOW THIS ROUND CLOSES. ***
351: #
352: #  I applied the survive-only-is-vacuous lesson to cnt=407 and NOT to the other two
353: #  ends of the same guard.  `zero surv` and (as first drafted) the over-length case
354: #  ask only "did the host survive?", and at this site that question is answerable
355: #  YES while the defect is fully restored:
356: #
357: #    EXEMPT-ZERO   `d->cnt != 408`  ->  `d->cnt != 408 && d->cnt != 0`
358: #        cnt=0 is then ACCEPTED -> alloc_data(0) -> malloc(0), both memsets become
359: #        no-ops, and the writer still stores through d->data[101*4+3].  **408 bytes
360: #        into a zero-byte allocation** -- the round's own �2 defect, back -- and a
361: #        write past a minimal chunk need not fault, so `zero surv` reads exit0.
362: #        Found by the glm seat on pass 2; predicted 15/15 clean before this row.
363: #
364: #    LOWER-BOUND   `d->cnt != 408`  ->  `d->cnt < 408`
365: #        cnt >= 409 is then ACCEPTED.  No overflow (409 >= 408), so nothing dies --
366: #        but the guard now admits lengths it must refuse, and NO SHIPPED ROW USED A
367: #        VALUE ABOVE 408.  Found independently by the grok and agy seats, both
368: #        predicting a 15/15 sweep.
369: #
370: #  Both are killed the same way the `> 408` mutant was: by asking the DECLINE
371: #  question on the COND channel instead of the survival question.
372: A = Addr(CODE)
373: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 0) + load16(GDROM_COND, A))
374: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
375: row("zero cond", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
376:     "0xa0", "DECLINE: cnt=0 declines -- kills the exempt-zero mutant")
377: 
378: A = Addr(CODE)
379: t4, alive4, how4 = session(stage_toc(A, 409))
380: row("over surv", how4, "exit0", "cnt=409 declines instead of ending the host")
381: 
382: A = Addr(CODE)
383: t, alive, how = session(stage_toc(A, 409) + load16(GDROM_COND, A))
384: g = printed(t, "r2") if alive else "died"
385: row("over cond", ("0x%x" % (int(g, 16) & 0xff)) if g.startswith("0x") else g,
386:     "0xa0", "DECLINE: cnt=409 declines -- kills the `< 408` mutant")
387: 
388: #  THE LATCH.  Two bad TOCs in one session must print exactly ONE diagnostic.
389: #  fatal() cannot be silenced by -q, so an unlatched guest-drivable message is a
390: #  flood; N=1 per device instance is the house idiom.
391: #  *** THE SECOND DECLINE IS READ BACK, AND THAT ROW IS THE ONE THAT KILLS THE
392: #  LATCH-STATE MUTANT.  Added after pass 2; found by ONE seat of nine. ***
393: #
394: #  `if (d->cnt != 408 && !(d->warned & GDROM_W_TOCLEN))` -- the nested-if collapse,
395: #  or equivalently a brace slip that moves `break` inside the latch block -- was
396: #  measured to sweep the 18-row version CLEAN while re-opening the heap overflow:
397: #
398: #    * every NON-latch row runs its own fresh session, where `warned` is clear, so
399: #      the mutant behaves identically to the correct build and every row passes;
400: #    * the latch session declines the FIRST bad TOC (setting the bit) and then
401: #      ACCEPTS the second, because the latch bit now gates the guard itself.  One
402: #      message is still printed and the host still survives, so `latch once` = 1
403: #      and `latch surv` = exit0 BOTH PASS -- while the second bad TOC reached
404: #      alloc_data(406) and the writer stored through byte 407.
405: #
406: #  *** SO THE GENERALISATION THE HEADER STATES IS ONE LEVEL SHORT. ***  It says
407: #  every VALUE the guard refuses needs a decline-channel row.  True -- and every
408: #  STATE in which it refuses needs one too.  The latched state had only a count row
409: #  and a survival row, which are exactly the two shapes this file already knows are
410: #  vacuous here.
411: #
