12: #        `len != 8' -> `len != 8 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE'  (the `lw surv' rows);
13: #      * ANY decline that does not reach the function's common tail -- an early
14: #        `return', or `return 0' (which memory_rw coerces to -1 and turns into a
15: #        guest EXCEPTION_DBE).  The `lw surv' rows check the VALUE the declined
16: #        read returns, not merely that the host survived: the destination
17: #        register is preset to a sentinel, so "tail ran, wrote 0" and "load
18: #        never completed" are distinguishable.  A survival-only oracle at a
19: #        refusal-effect site is vacuous by construction, and this row was one
20: #        until the d_returnzero mutant was built;
21: #      * deleting the latch, or dropping the `d->warned |= BIT' set, so the
22: #        message floods  (the `sw lat1' rows COUNT; they do not test presence);
23: #      * making two or more sites share a latch bit  (`xsite 6msg');
24: #      * mis-attributing a window's message to another window (`xsite order');
25: #      * growth of the width predicate that swallows LEGAL 8-byte accesses
26: #        (the `accept' rows, probed at an offset with bit 3 SET so that a
27: #        `|| (relative_addr & 8)' tightening reddens them);
28: #      * silent removal of the six READ kills or of the final catch-all, which
29: #        this round deliberately does NOT touch  (`<s> ld kill' x6, `other kill').
30: #
31: #    *** THE TWO PARAGRAPHS ABOVE ARE #497's AND #499's AND ARE NO LONGER TRUE OF THIS
32: #        FILE.  #513 REMOVED ALL SIX READ KILLS -- five arms became ACCEPTs and
33: #        CRIME_RE_TEX became a latched decline -- so `<s> ld kill' x6 NO LONGER EXIST
34: #        as row names, and an 8-byte `ld' to those windows no longer ends the host.
35: #        The contract below is the LIVE one; the text above is kept, with this
36: #        correction beside it, because a reader arriving from #497's or #499's records
37: #        needs to see which half moved and when.  Found by the pass-2 panel, which read
38: #        the contract against the diff and reported it stale. ***
39: #
40: #    *** AND IT PINS THE ROUND'S CENTRAL DISCLOSURE, RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT.
41: #        The width guard is evaluated FIRST, so after #497 a 4-byte read
42: #        DECLINES -- but an ordinary 8-byte `ld' to each of the six windows
43: #        STILL ENDS THE HOST, because len == 8 passes the width guard and falls
44: #        into the read arm's exit(1) (dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279 296 346
45: #        362).  Fixing six width kills leaves EXACTLY six read kills at the same
46: #        six offsets.  The `<s> ld kill' rows measure that at every window, so a
47: #        future reader cannot conclude from `lw surv' that reads were made safe.
48: #        Filed as sgirekills.  *** ITS WITNESS IS THIS FILE: the six `<site> ld kill`
49: #        rows below ARE rung-3 symptom demonstrations of that defect, and the ledger's
50: #        witness field names them.  This comment used to say the row `has no witness
51: #        yet' -- written before those rows existed and left standing after they shipped,
52: #        so the file denied being the witness it had become.  Found by the codex seat. ***
53: #
54: #    DOES NOT CATCH
55: #      * anything in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de) or (sgi_mte), including sgi_de's own
56: #        width kill (accept set {4,8}, filed sgidewidth) and sgi_mte's
57: #        unreachable one (filed sgimtewidth);
58: #      * the LINEAR out-of-bounds index itself.  Observing re_linear_b[0] being
59: #        clobbered needs a READ path, and every read arm here still exit(1)s.
60: #        *** THAT CLAUSE IS #497's AND IS FALSE SINCE #513: five read arms ACCEPT and
61: #        CRIME_RE_TEX declines, so a READ PATH NOW EXISTS.  Whether that makes the
62: #        aliasing rowable is a QUESTION FOR sgilinearoob, not a claim made here.
63: #        The aliasing is pinned by offsetof in PRETABLE.out section 1.2, not by
64: #        a row.  Owed to sgirekills.
65: #
66: #    ONE THING THE CONTRACT UNDERSTATED, corrected before shipping rather than
67: #    left as a modest-sounding false claim: an earlier draft said there is NO
68: #    anchored cell-position row.  There is.  Each `accept' row matches the arm's
69: #    own debug() text INCLUDING THE INDEX AND THE VALUE -- e.g.
70: #    "d->re_tlb_a[4] = 0x1122" -- so it is anchored to a SPECIFIC cell by a
71: #    channel outside the decode under test, which is clause (f)'s requirement.
72: #    A re-map of the index arithmetic (>> 1 -> >> 2) or of the byte packing
73: #    reddens it.  Window-level translations are additionally caught by the
74: #    per-site attribution rows, since each checks the message naming ITS OWN
75: #    symbol.  What remains genuinely uncovered is a re-map that preserves both
76: #    index and symbol.
77: #
78: import os, pty, re, select, sys, time
105: OUTSIDE     = 0xb5001800          # past LINEAR_B -> the final else (straggler)
106: ACC_VAL     = "0x1122334455667788"
107: 
108: #  *** THE RE-PIN VALUES (sgirekills).  VAL_HI differs from VAL_LO in every
109: #  halfword lane.
110: #
111: #  AN EARLIER WORDING CALLED THAT "the point of having two", claiming a single
112: #  value, or two sharing any lane, "would let such an edit through".  THAT
113: #  OVERCLAIMED, and the pass-2 panel caught it: VAL_LO ALONE already has four
114: #  DISTINCT halfwords (1122 3344 5566 7788), so it by itself reddens under a
115: #  pure read-side lane permutation or duplication -- M11_lanerev reds the lo
116: #  row as well as the hi one, which is the measurement that settles it.
117: #
118: #  What the SECOND value actually buys is narrower and still worth having: two
119: #  rows cannot both be satisfied by a mutant returning one fixed constant, and
120: #  the hi row exercises the window's LAST SLOT -- which is what M4 and M7
121: #  measured, and neither is a lane property at all. ***
122: VAL_LO      = 0x1122334455667788
123: VAL_HI      = 0xa1b2c3d4e5f60718
124: 
125: #  Each ACCEPT window gets TWO rows: one at the window BASE and one at its LAST
126: #  8-byte slot.  #499 measured on THIS DEVICE that one row per arm cannot do the
127: #  job -- an accept row written at a base tests the near bound twice and the far
128: #  bound never, and moving it to the top merely swaps which bound is blind.
129: #
130: #         nm          base          array          lo_off  lo_i  hi_off  hi_i  kind
131: REPIN = [
132:  ("tlb_a",    0xb5001000, "re_tlb_a",    0x000, 0, 0x1f8, 252, "hw"),
133:  ("tlb_b",    0xb5001200, "re_tlb_b",    0x000, 0, 0x1f8, 252, "hw"),
134:  ("tlb_c",    0xb5001400, "re_tlb_c",    0x000, 0, 0x1f8, 252, "hw"),
135:  ("linear_a", 0xb5001700, "re_linear_a", 0x000, 0, 0x078,  30, "w"),
136:  ("linear_b", 0xb5001780, "re_linear_b", 0x000, 0, 0x078,  30, "w"),
137: ]
138: 
139: TEX_BASE    = 0xb5001600
140: TEX_HI_OFF  = 0x0d8               # the last 8-byte slot inside CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0
141: TEX_RD_DECL = "unimplemented read from CRIME_RE_TEX -- declined."
142: 
143: def anchor(arr, idx, val, kind):
144:     """*** THE ANCHOR CHANNEL, clause (f). ***  The store's OWN debug() names the
145:     INDEX it wrote, so this literal pins WHICH CELL the address resolved to --
146:     not merely that some cell round-tripped.  A translation that slides the whole
147:     window keeps every value distinct and still reddens here.  Nothing is added to
148:     the READ path to make this work: the channel already exists on the write."""
149:     if kind == "hw":
150:         return "d->%s[%i] = 0x%04x" % (arr, idx, (val >> 48) & 0xffff)
151:     return "[ d->%s[%i] = 0x%08x, [%i] = 0x%08x ]" % (
152:         arr, idx, (val >> 32) & 0xffffffff, idx + 1, val & 0xffffffff)
153: 
281: 
282: def load(A, addr, op=OP_LW_V0_AT):
283:     return ["at=0x%x" % addr, "v0=0x%x" % SENTINEL] + run_ops([op], A.take(1))
284: 
285: print("#497 widthkillclass DETECTOR -- binary=%s label=%s" % (BIN, LABEL))
286: print("%-18s %-12s %-12s %-46s %s" % ("row", "got", "want", "note", ""))
287: 
288: # ------------------------------------------------ controls / identity  (c)
289: A = Addr(CODE)
290: t, alive, how = session(store(A, SCRATCH_RAM) + load(A, SCRATCH_RAM)
291:                         + ["print v0"])
292: row("live", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x%x" % LIVE_VAL,
293:     "LIVENESS: guest store then load, through RAM")
294: 
295: #  *** THE 64-BIT LIVENESS CONTROL, AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. ***  Every re-pinned
296: #  ACCEPT row below asserts a 64-bit value read back through `print v0'.  On the
297: #  COMMITTED build all of those rows DIE at the load, so a pre-fix run cannot tell
298: #  a correct want-string from a mis-formatted one -- the red is explained by the
299: #  kill either way.  This row exercises the identical sd/ld/print path through RAM,
300: #  where nothing kills, so the format is pinned BEFORE the fix rather than after.
301: A = Addr(CODE)
302: t, alive, how = session(store(A, SCRATCH_RAM, OP_SD_T0_A3, "0x%016x" % VAL_LO)
303:                         + load(A, SCRATCH_RAM, OP_LD_V0_AT) + ["print v0"])
304: row("live64", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", "0x%x" % VAL_LO,
305:     "LIVENESS: 64-bit sd/ld through RAM -- pins the re-pin want format")
306: 
307: A = Addr(CODE)
308: t, alive, how = session(load(A, DE_STATUS) + ["print v0"])
309: row("devsig stat", printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died", DE_STAT_VAL,
310:     "DEVSIG: CRIME_DE_STATUS reads 0x1e000000; RAM would read 0")
311: 
312: print("OPUS497T_CONTROL=%s" % ("OK" if all(o for _, o in rows) else "DEAD"))
313: print("")
314: 
315: # ------------------------------------------------ per site x 6
316: for nm, base, sym, acctext in SITES:
317:     lit = decl(sym)
318: 
319:     # 1. a len=4 WRITE must be DECLINED, not fatal
320:     A = Addr(CODE)
321:     t, alive, how = session(store(A, base))
322:     row(nm + " sw surv", how, "exit0", "len=4 WRITE declined, host survives")
323: 
324:     # 2. the latch: TWO declined accesses in ONE session -> exactly ONE message
325:     A = Addr(CODE)
326:     t, alive, how = session(store(A, base) + store(A, base + 4))
327:     row(nm + " sw lat1", t.count(lit), 1,
328:         "(once): COUNT over 2 accesses -- presence would pass unlatched")
329: 
330:     # 3. a len=4 READ must ALSO be declined.
331:     #    NOTE: the width arm is evaluated BEFORE the read arm, so a len=4 read
332:     #    takes the (now declining) width arm and never reaches the still-live
333:     #    read kill.  An 8-byte read still dies -- see `ld readkill' below.
334:     #    *** BOTH HALVES FALSE SINCE #513: the 8-byte read is ACCEPTED (or, at TEX,
335:     #    declined), and `ld readkill' no longer exists -- it is `<arm> ld lo|hi'.
336:     #    This row is what kills M1 (`len != 8 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE').
337:     A = Addr(CODE)
338:     t, alive, how = session(load(A, base) + ["print v0"])
339:     got = how + "/" + (printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died")
340:     row(nm + " lw surv", got, "exit0/0x0",
341:         "declined READ reaches the tail and returns a DEFINED 0")
342: 
343:     # 4. ACCEPT side (de-escalation clause).  Offset +8 has bit 3 SET on
344:     #    purpose: a `|| (relative_addr & 8)' growth must redden this.
345:     A = Addr(CODE)
346:     t, alive, how = session(store(A, base + 8, OP_SD_T0_A3, ACC_VAL))
347:     row(nm + " accept", "1" if acctext in t else "0", "1",
348:         "LEGAL len=8 write still lands (probed at +8, bit 3 set)")
349: 
350: # ------------------------------------------------ cross-site  (M2 + anchor)
351: A = Addr(CODE)
352: cmds = []
353: for nm, base, sym, _a in SITES:
354:     cmds += store(A, base)
355: t, alive, how = session(cmds, spin=2.0)
356: present = [decl(sym) for _n, _b, sym, _a in SITES]
357: n_present = sum(1 for lit in present if t.count(lit) == 1)
358: row("xsite 6msg", n_present, 6,
359:     "six windows, ONE session: 6 distinct latches (kills bit collision)")
360: 
361: pos = [t.find(lit) for lit in present]
362: ordered = all(p >= 0 for p in pos) and pos == sorted(pos)
363: row("xsite order", "1" if ordered else "0", "1",
364:     "ANCHOR: message order == the order the guest issued them")
365: 
366: # ------------------------------------------------ the read arms  (sgirekills)
367: #
368: #  *** RE-PINNED FOR sgirekills.  SIX DEATH-ONLY ROWS BECOME FOURTEEN. ***
369: #
370: #  THE OLD ROWS were `row(nm + " ld kill", how + txt, "exit1+txt")' -- they asserted
371: #  that a len == 8 READ still ends the host at each of the six windows.  That was a
372: #  true and useful pin while the kills stood.  It cannot survive the fix, which is
373: #  exactly the situation the oracle-correction rule governs: the rows are re-pinned
374: #  FIRST and the committed build is measured RED under the new expectations, and only
375: #  then is the device edited.  Same script, opposite colour.
376: #
377: #  *** WHAT REPLACED THEM, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE OBVIOUS SET. ***  My first design was
378: #  one row per window at the window BASE, plus a #499-style decline row for TEX.  The
379: #  design panel refused it with two floor-shaped holes and one oracle this file had
380: #  already measured wrong:
381: #
382: #    (1) THE FAR BOUND WAS UNPINNED.  An accept row at a window's base tests the near
383: #        bound twice and the far bound never -- so a shrink of the window's upper limit
384: #        is invisible to it.  #499 measured that on this same device.  Hence TWO rows
385: #        per ACCEPT arm, base and LAST SLOT.
386: #
387: #    (2) A LOAD-ONLY TEX DECLINE ROW WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, and this is the sharp one.

exec
